course: dictionary
lesson: 9304
title: Being specific
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Specific means exact, particular and clear. It names one certain thing, not things in general. «A specific time» is 3 o'clock, not «later». «A specific example» is one clear case, not a vague idea.
explain: Writers and speakers use it to make their meaning sharp. «Parents often praise everything because they love you, even when the praise is not specific.» Vague praise like "good job" tells you little, but specific praise names the exact thing that was good.
explain: The word has a second meaning: specific to something means belonging only to that thing or place. Before you finish your work, ask yourself: could a reader point to exactly what I mean?
fact: Specific means exact, particular and clear.
fact: «Be specific» means name the exact thing, number or reason instead of a vague one.
fact: «The dog» is specific, but «it» is vague. «At 3 o'clock» is specific, but «later» is vague.
fact: Specific to something means belonging only to that thing or place.
fact: In an emergency, give specific information: the exact address and what happened.
exercise: «A good compliment is specific and true.» What does specific mean in this sentence? | *Exact and clear, naming the real thing | Rude or unkind | Very long and difficult | Quiet and secret
exercise: Your teacher asks when the bell rings. Which answer is more specific? | *At 3 o'clock | Soon | Sometime | Later
exercise: «Plants specific to the rainforest» grow where? | Everywhere in the world | Near the sea | In a garden at home | *Only in the rainforest
test: Which is a specific example? | A rough idea of something | Lots of things, in general | *One clear case: the bus left at 8.15 | Something nice
test: Which is the best call for help? | Please come soon | Someone is here | Something bad happened somewhere | *Send an ambulance to 12 Mill Road, a man has fallen off a ladder
exam: Which is the best way to answer a teacher's question? | *Name the exact thing, number or reason | Say nothing at all | Give vague answers like "it" or "later" | Answer with a question
